  <!--
 VECTORS
  
  Transmission.
  - Injury, Contact, Ingested, and Inhaled, Bites?

  Airborne
  Tiny droplet nuclei or dust stay in the air for a long time and travel far
  on air currents. Examples include measles and tuberculosis.

  Fomites (surfaces): Germs live on non-living objects like doorknobs, phones,
  or toys until touched by a new host. Examples include norovirus and the common
  cold.

  Vehicles: Contaminated food, water, or blood products carry pathogens to
  multiple people. Examples include cholera and hepatitis A.

  Vectors: Living creatures—mostly insects like mosquitoes, ticks, and
  fleas—carry pathogens between hosts. Examples include malaria and Lyme
  disease.

-->

